 ## Overview
-Hard Voxelization (Deterministic) discretizes a continuous 3D point cloud into a fixed-size 3D grid of cubic voxels by binning points based on their spatial coordinates and aggregating associated attribute values within each voxel [S3, unofficial]. It outputs a single representative point per occupied voxel calculated as the centroid of the aggregated attributes [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Multiscale Hard Voxelization](3319-multiscale-hard-voxelization.md), which handles multiple resolutions, this method operates on a single fixed scale [S2, unofficial]. It differs from the non-deterministic variant by guaranteeing reproducible output through explicit sorting and unique-coordinate identification, ensuring that the order of points within a voxel is preserved, whereas non-deterministic versions rely on atomic operations that may yield varying results across runs [S3, unofficial].
+Hard Voxelization (Deterministic) converts a continuous 3D point cloud into a fixed-size discrete voxel grid by quantizing coordinates, aggregating point attributes (e.g., intensity, color) per voxel, and outputting a representative point (typically the centroid) for each occupied voxel [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of downsampling and structuring unstructured point clouds for efficient processing in deep learning or rendering pipelines [S1, S3, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Fast Deterministic GPU Voxelization (Sort-Scatter)](3806-fast-deterministic-gpu-voxelization-sort-scatter.md), which uses a sort-scatter pipeline for massive speedups, this method relies on explicit sorting or unique-coordinate identification to guarantee bit-identical, reproducible output order regardless of hardware parallelism [S3, unofficial]. It differs from [Grid Pooling (Max Pooling over Points)](4327-grid-pooling-max-pooling-over-points.md) by computing centroids (means) rather than max-pooling, and from [Voxel Hashing and Mean Aggregation](11234-voxel-hashing-and-mean-aggregation.md) by using a pre-allocated dense grid structure rather than sparse hashing [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method maps continuous point coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to discrete integer voxel indices $(i, j, k)$ using floor division relative to a bounding box origin and voxel dimensions [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Inputs and Outputs
-**Inputs:**
-- `points`: A 2D array of shape $(N, D)$, where $N$ is the number of points and $D$ is the number of features [S2, S3, unofficial].
-- `voxel_size`: A 3-element array $[v_x, v_y, v_z]$ indicating the physical size of a voxel in x, y, and z dimensions [S3, unofficial].
-- `coors_range`: A 6-element array $[x_{min}, y_{min}, z_{min}, x_{max}, y_{max}, z_{max}]$ defining the bounding box of the voxel grid [S3, unofficial].
-- `max_points`: Integer. Maximum number of points allowed in a single voxel. If -1, dynamic voxelization is used [S3, unofficial].
-- `max_voxels`: Integer. Maximum number of voxels to create. Points falling into voxels beyond this limit are dropped. Typical value is 20000 [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Outputs:**
-- `voxels`: A tensor of shape $[M, \text{max\_points}, D]$, where $M$ is the number of valid voxels ($\le \text{max\_voxels}$). It stores the features of the points assigned to each voxel [S3, unofficial].
-- `coors`: A tensor of shape $[M, 3]$ containing the integer voxel indices $(i, j, k)$ for each of the $M$ voxels [S3, unofficial].
-- `num_points_per_voxel`: A tensor of shape $[M]$ storing the number of points assigned to each voxel [S3, unofficial].
-
